<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n87519" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">προθέω</span></b>  (B), 3pl.
		<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">προθέουσιν,</span> sts. taken as a form of
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">προτίθημι,</span> found once in <i><span class="en">
			Hom.
		</span></i>, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τοὔνεκά οἱ προθέουσιν ὀνείδεα
			μυθήσασθαι;</span>
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>do they</i> therefore
				<i>appoint</i> for him revilings to utter? <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				1.291
			</span></i> (but expld. fr. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">προθέω</span> (A)
			by <i><span class="en">
				Aristarch.
			</span></i>, with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀνείδεα</span> as
				subj.; perh., <i>do</i> his insults <i>dash
				forward</i> for utterance?).</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
